---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-hives`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Chronic Hives (Urticaria)',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Chronic urticaria', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'L50', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Hives', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Chronic Hives (Urticaria): Is There Anything to It?"
  description="Chronic spontaneous urticaria is often immune-driven. Small trials suggest add-on vitamin D may modestly reduce hive scores, but antihistamines remain first-line. The evidence, weighed."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-hives", label: "Vitamin D & Hives" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Chronic Hives (Urticaria)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Chronic spontaneous urticaria — itchy hives (wheals) appearing most days for more than six weeks
      without an obvious trigger — is a frustrating, often immune-mediated condition. Because vitamin D
      modulates immune cells, including the mast cells that drive hives, it has been studied as an
      add-on. The evidence is intriguing but modest.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The rationale and the association</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D influences mast-cell stability, regulatory T cells, and inflammatory signalling — all
      relevant to urticaria. Several case-control studies report lower 25(OH)D in people with chronic
      urticaria than in controls, and some find lower vitamin D correlates with more severe or
      longer-lasting disease. As ever, association is not proof: chronic illness and indoor living
      lower vitamin D too.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the add-on trials show</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A number of small randomised and controlled studies have tested vitamin D <em>in addition to</em>
      antihistamines. A frequently cited trial (Rorie 2014) found that adding high-dose vitamin D
      (4,000 IU/day) to standard therapy produced a modest but statistically significant reduction in
      urticaria symptom scores compared with a lower dose over 12 weeks. Other small trials and
      meta-analyses point in the same direction — a small improvement in symptom scores when vitamin D
      is added — but the studies are heterogeneous, often low quality, and the effect size is not large.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where it sits in treatment</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      First-line treatment for chronic urticaria is second-generation (non-sedating) antihistamines,
      up-titrated to as much as four times the standard dose if needed. When that is insufficient,
      guidelines add omalizumab (an anti-IgE biologic) or other agents. Vitamin D is, at most, a
      low-cost <em>adjunct</em> — reasonable to correct if you are deficient and to trial alongside
      proper treatment, but not a replacement for antihistamines and not something to megadose in the
      hope of a cure.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Antihistamines first, at the dose your clinician recommends — this is where the strong evidence is.</li>
      <li>Ask about checking 25(OH)D; correct a genuine deficiency.</li>
      <li>Adding vitamin D is low-risk and may modestly help symptom scores in some people.</li>
      <li>Keep doses sensible — the trials used ordinary supplemental amounts, not extreme megadoses.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Hives with lip or tongue swelling, difficulty breathing, or
      faintness may signal anaphylaxis and need emergency care. Persistent chronic hives deserve proper
      assessment rather than supplement-only self-treatment.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-hives" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
